WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.429
INTRO:
With every passing year, it seems like more

00:00:02.429 --> 00:00:07.319
and more physical retailers fall off the map
as they struggle to keep up with the online

00:00:07.319 --> 00:00:09.150
revolution.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:14.970
Retailers as big as JC Penny, J Crew, Neimen
Marcus, Pier 1 Imports, Toys R US, Fry's Electronics,

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:19.680
and even Sears have all filed for bankruptcy
or shut down completely.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.850
And with the surge of the pandemic, it seemed
like this trend would only accelerate, and

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:29.510
that’s exactly what happened throughout
2020 as dying retailers completely collapsed.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:34.190
But, as these weak retailers left the space,
the surviving retailers have emerged stronger

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:35.540
than ever before.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:41.911
In fact, according to the US government, in
2021, retail sales grew by 18% while online

00:00:41.911 --> 00:00:43.999
sales only grew by 14%.

00:00:43.999 --> 00:00:49.289
Now, you could chalk this up to a rebound
from pandemic lows, and I’m sure that plays

00:00:49.289 --> 00:00:50.289
a role.

00:00:50.289 --> 00:00:54.730
But, this confirms that there are certain
products that people prefer to buy in person

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.050
even if they’re available online.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:00.989
Otherwise, why would consumers have gone back
to physical retailers?

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:05.980
Also, given that a bunch of physical retailers
shut down during the pandemic, you would think

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:10.000
that it would be harder for the remaining
retailers to make a comeback, but that doesn’t

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.030
seem to be the case.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:16.760
In fact, the National Retail Federation predicts
that 72% of US retail sales will still occur

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.990
in brick and mortar stores in 2024.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:25.980
So, why are physical retailers making a comeback
and is this sustainable or just a fluke.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:31.310
DELIVERY &amp; RETURN:
One of the biggest strengths of physical retailers

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:34.020
is their delivery and return process.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.990
While Amazon has continuously pushed down
delivery times from weeks to days to hours,

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:43.350
there’s often still quite a bit of friction
within these processes.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.160
First of all, you have the package misdelivery
problem.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.450
I’m sure all of us have received one of
our neighbors packages or our packages have

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.170
ended up at our neighbors house.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:57.600
And oftentimes, you don’t even know where
your package went and it's just gone forever.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.800
Whenever this happens, you have to file a
complaint with the retailer and UPS or Fedex,

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.340
and it’s just a massive headache.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.659
In fact, I bet a lot of consumers don’t
even file lost package complaints especially

00:02:08.659 --> 00:02:11.319
for cheap products just to avoid the hassle.

00:02:11.319 --> 00:02:15.129
While this is simply a minor annoyance for
such cheap products, where this becomes a

00:02:15.129 --> 00:02:17.719
serious problem is expensive products.

00:02:17.719 --> 00:02:22.549
I don’t think any of us want to deal with
a new smartphone or laptop being delivered

00:02:22.549 --> 00:02:24.459
to some random dude in the city.

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:29.540
Now of course, there is the option of requesting
a signed delivery for expensive products,

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.650
but this isn’t necessarily all that convenient.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:36.900
You now have to spend your entire day at home
waiting for the delivery guy.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:42.150
There’s also the problem of porch pirates
which is especially a problem within big cities.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:48.659
In fact, over 10% of all packages disappear
without an explanation in the major US cities.

00:02:48.659 --> 00:02:53.569
And given that most of us make at least one
online purchase every month, we’re talking

00:02:53.569 --> 00:02:57.189
about dealing with at least one lost package
every year.

00:02:57.189 --> 00:02:59.420
And then there’s the problem of returns.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:04.700
Historically, returning products has been
one of the biggest hassles of the online shopping

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:05.700
experience.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:11.459
26% of online shoppers say that they never
return an online order and 65% say that they

00:03:11.459 --> 00:03:13.760
rarely return an online order.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.870
This is quite ironic when you consider that
online return rates are still way higher than

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:19.439
brick and mortar return rates.

00:03:19.439 --> 00:03:25.420
On average, shoppers return 5-10% of in store
purchases while shoppers return 15 to 40%

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:27.360
of online purchases.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.610
This means that if most consumers weren't
too lazy to return online orders, this number

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:33.340
would be even higher.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.540
Meanwhile, the successful physical retailers
have figured out how to perfectly combine

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:42.230
the online and physical shopping experiences
to offer the best of both worlds.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:46.090
For example, with most stores, you can do
curbside pickup.

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:50.859
This is perfect if you wanna order something
online but don’t want to deal with delivery.

00:03:50.859 --> 00:03:54.809
With curbside, you don’t even have to get
out of your car, but you can easily ensure

00:03:54.809 --> 00:03:58.739
that your package doesn’t go to someone
else and that it’s the right product.

00:03:58.739 --> 00:04:03.809
Also, it’s not a hassle to pick up the product
either if you plan it right.

00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:07.159
You can just pick up your purchase when you
come back from work.

00:04:07.159 --> 00:04:10.889
Similarly, if you don’t want the product,
you can simply drop it off in store the next

00:04:10.889 --> 00:04:12.830
day after work.

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:17.299
Considering this, I believe a lot of Americans
will prefer curbside pickup even over home

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:18.400
delivery.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.229
And as the convenience disparity between physical
and online shopping shrinks, physical retailers

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:26.330
will simply continue to make up ground.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:31.180
ON DEMAND PURCHASES:
Aside from physical retailers shining in the

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.580
delivery and return categories, physical retailers
will continue to kill it when it comes to

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.199
on demand products like groceries.

00:04:38.199 --> 00:04:42.340
While we have services like InstaCart and
WholeFoods home delivery, there’s no doubt

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:45.580
that these options are still extremely expensive.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:49.930
And most Americans are unwilling to pay such
high delivery fees to simply receive everyday

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:51.440
goods like groceries.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.060
While this has always been the case, it wasn’t
till recently that physical retailers have

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.990
started to leverage this advantage specifically
with curbside grocery delivery.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:04.919
Now, this isn’t as advantageous for retailers
that only sell groceries, but this is extremely

00:05:04.919 --> 00:05:10.060
helpful for mega retailers like Walmart, Target,
Sam's Club, and Costco who sell everything

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:11.520
under the sun.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.300
Let’s say you need a new mouse.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:17.550
Instead of ordering it from Amazon, why not
bundle it in with a Walmart grocery order

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.050
that you’re gonna pickup later that afternoon.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:24.050
Not only would you receive the product earlier,
but you didn’t even have to do anything

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:25.050
extra.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:28.199
After all, you would’ve gone to Walmart
to pick up your groceries whether or not you

00:05:28.199 --> 00:05:29.889
ordered the mouse.

00:05:29.889 --> 00:05:35.069
Another category that won't be going online
anytime soon is home improvement items.

00:05:35.069 --> 00:05:38.879
If you’re installing a cabinet and run out
of screws, it makes a lot more sense to simply

00:05:38.879 --> 00:05:43.270
make a quick trip to Home Depot or Lowes instead
of trying to order screws from Amazon.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:47.879
Also, when you go to the store in person,
you can usually consult with Home Depot employees

00:05:47.879 --> 00:05:49.919
who are generally quite knowledgeable.

00:05:49.919 --> 00:05:54.230
Home Depot literally hires professional plumbers,
carpenters, and electricians to help customers

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:56.430
find products and give them tips on installation.

00:05:56.430 --> 00:06:01.879
This has continued to be an invaluable service,
and if you don’t believe me, just take a

00:06:01.879 --> 00:06:03.460
look at Home Depot stock.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:08.460
While they have cooled down recently, they’re
stock has been going parabolic for years.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:12.470
And as physical retailers continue to embrace
such on demand categories, we’ll likely

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:16.889
see consumers spend more money at these retailers
than ever before.

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:22.400
PRICE GAP:
On top of struggling with some inherent shortfalls,

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:26.090
online retailers have also been losing some
of the key advantages that made them better

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:28.140
in the first place like price.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:32.770
About 10 years ago, a phenomenon called showrooming
was extremely common.

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:36.199
This is when consumers go to a store to check
out a product and then go home to purchase

00:06:36.199 --> 00:06:38.880
the product online because it's cheaper.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:43.370
While this made a lot of sense a decade ago,
it’s not as applicable today given that

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:46.479
physical retailers have managed to close the
price gap.

00:06:46.479 --> 00:06:50.840
Some retailers even offer special in store
discounts which simply further incentivizes

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:52.139
in store shopping.

00:06:52.139 --> 00:06:56.880
And this makes sense given that home delivery
is not cheap by any means.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:00.919
Amazon had to run a money losing operation
for decades in order to eventually achieve

00:07:00.919 --> 00:07:02.981
the economies of scale required to make home
delivery profitable.

00:07:02.981 --> 00:07:08.289
And with time, Amazon has only increased the
cost of their prime subscription which now

00:07:08.289 --> 00:07:10.680
sits at $140.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:14.979
Aside from charging consumers for delivery,
Amazon also charges sellers for fulfillment

00:07:14.979 --> 00:07:16.060
as well.

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:20.909
Amazon sellers generally have to pay $3 to
$6 for each item Amazon fulfills depending

00:07:20.909 --> 00:07:22.389
on its size.

00:07:22.389 --> 00:07:26.720
And as you would guess, sellers are just gonna
pass on this cost to consumers.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:31.199
Now, you can’t really blame Amazon for these
fees given that their net margin is in the

00:07:31.199 --> 00:07:32.430
single digits.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:36.740
However, this gives physical retailers the
perfect opportunity to be competitive in terms

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:39.689
of price and that’s exactly what they’ve
done.

00:07:39.689 --> 00:07:44.030
Walmart has actually overtaken Amazon in terms
of cheapness in several categories including

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:47.090
baby supplies, cleaning supplies, medicine,
and personal hygiene.

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:50.330
Amazon has maintained a lead in some other
categories such as make up, toys, and pet

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:51.860
food.

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:57.669
Meanwhile, in some categories such as snack
and food prices, Walmart and Amazon offer

00:07:57.669 --> 00:07:59.900
identical prices to the penny.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:04.340
So, clearly, it’s no longer a case of “It’s
always cheaper online.”

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:08.330
Usually, it depends on the product category,
and in the long term you’re not really going

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:11.949
to save money by choosing one or the other
in terms of product prices.

00:08:11.949 --> 00:08:15.789
However, by shopping at physical retailers,
you don’t have to pay an annual subscription

00:08:15.789 --> 00:08:20.810
fee or delivery fees, so it could actually
be cheaper to shop in person.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:26.479
SHOPPING EXPERIENCE:
And finally as for the last factor driving

00:08:26.479 --> 00:08:30.110
forward physical retailers is the shopping
experience itself.

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:34.729
Nowadays, people have gotten lazier than ever
before and have arguably gotten spoiled with

00:08:34.729 --> 00:08:35.729
home delivery.

00:08:35.729 --> 00:08:40.159
But, even despite this, there is something
special about in person shopping that you

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:42.849
just can’t get with online shopping.

00:08:42.849 --> 00:08:46.600
I’m not talking about weekly grocery shopping
or essential purchases.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:50.580
Rather, I’m talking about purchases that
you’ve been saving up for and are looking

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:51.630
forward to.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.060
Take the iPhone for example.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:57.320
8 years ago, I remember watching iJustine
camping outside of an Apple store to be one

00:08:57.320 --> 00:08:59.900
of the first people in the world to get the
iPhone 6.

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:04.570
With the rise of online shopping, Apple has
made it possible to simply pre order the iPhone

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:07.620
and get it delivered to your house on the
day it’s released.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:11.410
But, having the phone delivered to your doorstep
simply doesn’t have the same excitement

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:13.959
and satisfaction as buying it in store.

00:09:13.959 --> 00:09:19.930
Now, that’s of course an extreme example,
but this applies to smaller purchases as well.

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:23.320
Buying shoes online isn’t the same as going
to a Nike store with your friends.

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:27.839
And I believe most consumers feel the same
way given that the majority of shoppers still

00:09:27.839 --> 00:09:32.430
prefer to shop in store, and here’s the
reasons they cite for this preference.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:36.750
33% of respondents to this survey suggest
that they prefer to view, touch, and interact

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:38.130
with physical products.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:42.230
26% of respondents suggest that they enjoy
the overall shopping experience that a physical

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:44.050
location provides.

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:48.310
And 13% of respondents suggest that they like
the immediacy that in store shopping provides.

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:52.200
Hearing this, physical retailers have placed
a significant emphasis on improving the in

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:56.200
store experience from buffing up customer
support to making the entire process seamless.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:00.290
And it seems like these improvements have
been working given that consumers still prefer

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:02.330
in store shopping in general.

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:07.790
THE CASE OF PHYSICAL RETAILERS:
Throughout this entire video, we’ve discussed

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:12.730
the various advantages of physical retailers
and the biggest shortfalls of online retailers.

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:16.540
And though online retailers do have their
disadvantages, I don’t want to give off

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:19.730
the impression that onlines retailers are
doing poorly.

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:24.450
People are spending more money than ever before
online, and this will only increase moving

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:25.450
forward.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:29.490
But, with that being said, I also don’t
think that innovative retailers are going

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:30.490
anywhere either.

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:34.610
In fact, I think they perform tremendously
as well.

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:38.200
The problem with physical retailers 10 years
ago was that they were just simply trying

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:40.029
to replicate Amazon.

00:10:40.029 --> 00:10:44.279
They simply threw together a website and started
shipping products to people’s houses.

00:10:44.279 --> 00:10:48.360
This wasn’t very successful given that physical
retailers weren’t offering anything unique

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:51.320
or special in comparison to Amazon.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:55.140
But over the years, the best retailers have
learned to leverage their physical locations

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:57.760
to offer something that Amazon can’t.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:02.220
This includes a pick up and return convenience,
immediate availability, and a unique shopping

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:03.220
experience.

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:07.010
And as long as physical retailers continue
to lean into the factors that make them unique,

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:08.839
I don’t think they’ll be going anywhere.

00:11:08.839 --> 00:11:10.800
But, that’s just what I think.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:14.110
Do you guys prefer online shopping or in store
shopping?

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:15.350
Comment that down below.

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:19.430
Also, drop a like if you wish your packages
didn’t get lost in the mail on a regular

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:20.690
basis.

